      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "To evaluate $i^{11} + i^{111}$, we first need to understand the powers of the imaginary unit $i$. The powers of $i$ cycle every four terms: $i^1 = i$, $i^2 = -1$, $i^3 = -i$, and $i^4 = 1$. This pattern repeats for higher powers of $i$. So, we can determine the value of $i^{11}$ by finding the remainder when 11 is divided by 4. \n\n\\[\n11 \\div 4 = 2 \\text{ remainder } 3\n\\]\n\nThus, $i^{11} = i^3 = -i$. \n\nSimilarly, to find $i^{111}$, we determine the remainder when 111 is divided by 4:\n\n\\[\n111 \\div 4 = 27 \\text{ remainder } 3\n\\]\n\nTherefore, $i^{111} = i^3 = -i$.\n\nNow we can add the two results:\n\n\\[\ni^{11} + i^{111} = -i + (-i) = -2i\n\\]\n\nSo, the final answer is $\\boxed{-2i}$.",
